note Keeping the open window honest about what is on disk without ever stalling a frame. A watcher thread owns the indexed files' modification times and compares them against the disk once a second, so the main thread never stats the tree; it sends one message when something moved and then waits for the refreshed list. The frame itself only checks the map's files through map::stamp: a map written by the CLI is reloaded silently when there is nothing unsaved, and with unsaved edits it refuses and says so once, because saving would overwrite it. A map on disk that does not read, a merge conflict in it for instance, keeps the one in memory and stops saves until it reads. A changed source file starts a rebuild on another thread; the index in use is untouched until the new one arrives, and taking it kills every symbol index in the app - so the focused symbol, the pinned peek, the current file and everything the reader opened in the graph are carried across by (file path, symbol name) identity and the map's anchors are re-resolved. Neither kind of reload is a navigation: the selection is kept where the new map still supports it and the graph's camera does not move. Files that were created or deleted are not noticed; that needs a restart.

